content.text:
Small title looks good, though 2D badge detached. Fine.

Need fix small landscape pause screen (and likely fail/end screens) for short height. The CSS media query `@media (max-height: 570px) and (orientation: landscape)` currently only adjusts game HUD. We can add styles to scale down pause/fail/end cards and allow scrolling if necessary.

Options:
- Add to landscape media query:
```
.pause-card { transform: scale(.82); transform-origin: center; }
```
But transform scaling doesn't change layout, still overflow but visual fits? The card is centered via screen flex; transform origin center; at height 320, card 399*0.8=319, fits exactly. Top y maybe (320-399)/2 + (399-319)/2 = -39.5+40 = 0.5. Good. But text may be small. Better reduce padding/font sizes instead.
- Use `.screen { overflow-y: auto; }` and `align-items: flex-start` for landscape? But touch-action none on body and overflow hidden. Could allow scrolling, but game screens are overlays. On short landscape, user can't scroll easily if overflow hidden. Better make cards compact.

For pause card at 320h: need height <= 300 to leave margin. Current 399. Need reduce ~100px. We can scale to 0.75 (299px). Set `transform: scale(.75);` and maybe `transform-origin: center center;`. Since the screen flex centers it, scaled visual is centered. That works and keeps all content visible. At 390h landscape, card 399*0.75=299, fits with 45px margins. But it might look small on 390h. Could use `scale(.85)` at max-height 570: 339px, fits 390. At 320h, 339 > 320, still clipped. Use media query max-height 400 for scale .72. Or use CSS `max-height: calc(100vh - 24px); overflow-y: auto;` on `.pause-card`; that would make it scrollable within card, but user might not know and touch-action none prevents scroll? We can set `touch-action: pan-y` on the card. But simpler scale.

Use two breakpoints:
```
@media (max-height: 570px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card, .end-card { max-height: calc(100vh - 20px); overflow-y: auto; }
}
@media (max-height: 420px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card { padding: 16px 24px 14px; }
  .pause-card h2 { font-size: 34px; margin: 4px 0 12px; }
  .pause-stats { margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .pause-stats div { padding: 7px 8px; }
  .pause-card .primary-btn { padding: 11px 14px; }
  .pause-help { display: none; }
  .end-card { padding: 18px 24px; }
  .end-card h2 { font-size: 42px; }
  .end-stats { margin: 12px 0; }
  .fail-card h2 { font-size: 26px; }
  .fail-kicker { font-size: 64px; }
}
```
Need calculate pause height after compacting:
Current 399 includes:
- padding top 26? at max-width 900 `.pause-card { padding: 26px 24px 22px; }` -> 48 vertical
- kicker 8px line + h2 (clamp 40px..61px, at 568 width maybe 40? Actually h2 font clamp(40px,6vw,61px) = 40px, line-height .88, 2 lines -> 70px) + margins 7+22 = 99
- stats 1 row height ~? 11+? 41px + margin 24 = 65
- primary button ~? 40px + margin 9 = 49
- text buttons sound 30 + restart 30 = 60
- pause-help 21 margin + 13 padding + ~15 = 49
Total ~48+99+65+49+60+49=370, plus maybe. Current 399.
If hide pause-help (49) and reduce h2 font to 34 (2 lines 60 vs 70) and margins, reduce padding to 30, stats margin 12, buttons smaller, we can get ~260. Good.

But rather than compact all, a transform scale is easiest and preserves design. At 320h, scale .75; at 390h, .85. Use:
```
@media (max-height: 420px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card { transform: scale(.72); transform-origin: center; }
  .end-card { transform: scale(.72); }
  .fail-card { transform: scale(.8); }
}
```
But scaled cards may be too small to read. At 320h, 0.72 -> 287px tall, text 34px h2 etc. Readable. At 390h (iPhone landscape), max-height 420 applies, scale .72 -> 287, leaving 100px empty; too small. We can set .82 for max-height 420: 327px, fits 320? no. 399*.82=327 > 320, slightly clipped. Use .78: 311px, fits. At 390, 311 leaves 79px. Fine. So scale .78 for max-height 420. For max-height 340 maybe .7. Hmm.

Alternatively use `max-height: calc(100vh - 16px)` with `overflow-y: auto` and `scrollbar` hidden. The card content can scroll, but initial view still clipped. Not ideal.

Maybe use CSS `zoom` property? Not standard in Firefox, but Chrome supports. `zoom: 0.75` affects layout and centers properly. Safari supports zoom too. But not standard. Transform is safer.

Could add `.pause-screen { align-items: center; }` (already screen flex center). Transform scale visually centers around card's layout center, which is at viewport center if card height > viewport? The card is centered by flex, so its layout center = viewport center even if taller. Transform origin center keeps center. So with scale .78, visual top = 160 - 399*.78/2 = 160-155.6=4.4; bottom=315.6. Fits. Good.

For fail card: current 304 tall at 390 viewport; at 320h, 304 fits with 8px margins; but the fail card might be 304 at 320 too, fits barely. It also has kicker 62-130px, maybe 304. At 320, top 8, bottom 312, okay. But at 320x568 landscape? We tested 568x320; fail card 354x304, fits. End card 393 tall at 390 viewport; at 320h, 393 > 320, clipped. Need scale end card too. Pause and end scale .78. Fail maybe .9. Also the title screen on small landscape? We saw startBox at y203, h36, title fits (logo 12vh=38px). Fine.

Let's add to the existing landscape media query:
```
.pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(.78); }
.fail-card { transform: scale(.92); }
```
But at 390h, .78 makes pause card 311, a bit small but okay. Could use `@media (max-height: 430px) ... scale(.78)` and for 431-570 scale(.9). At 390h, 399*.9=359, fits 390 with 15px margin. At 320h, 399*.78=311, fits. So:
```
@media (max-height: 570px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(.9); }
}
@media (max-height: 380px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(.76); }
  .fail-card { transform: scale(.92); }
}
```
But transform on `.end-card` with width min(650, calc(100vw - 36px)) -> at 568x320, width 532, height 393*.9=354 > 320, still clipped. At .76 -> 299 fits. Good. At 844x390, end card width 650, height 393*.9=354 fits 390. Good.
Pause at 844x390: height 399*.9=359 fits. Good.
Fail at 844x390: height 304, fine. At 320h, 304, fine. So only scale end/pause at max-height 380.

But at 320h, pause 399*.76=303 fits with 8px margins. Good.
At 568x320, end 393*.76=299. Good.

Could also reduce `.pause-card` padding for short heights to avoid scaling, but transform is simpler and looks consistent.

Let's add:
```
@media (max-height: 570px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(0.9); }
}
@media (max-height: 380px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(0.76); }
}
```
But `.screen` uses flex center; transform scale keeps centered. Good.

However, the pause card content may be too small at .76 on 320h, but still readable at 282px width. The pause help may be tiny (7px * .76 = 5.3px), but acceptable for a rare screen. Could hide pause help at short heights instead of scaling all:
```
@media (max-height: 380px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-help { display:none; }
  .pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(.86); }
}
```
Hiding help reduces card height by ~49 -> 350, scale .86=301. Good. But the card's layout height is still 350, scale .86=301. Great. Let's do this. At max-height 380, hide pause-help and scale .86. At 320h, 350*.86=301. At 390h, max-height 380 doesn't apply, scale .9 -> 359. Good.
At 320h end card 393*.86=338 > 320; need .78. Hmm. End card has no pause help. Scale .78. So:
```
@media (max-height: 380px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  .pause-help { display: none; }
  .pause-card { transform: scale(.88); }  // 350*.88=308
  .end-card { transform: scale(.78); }    // 393*.78=306
  .fail-card { transform: scale(.92); }   // 304*.92=280
}
```
And at max-height 570:
```
.pause-card, .end-card { transform: scale(.9); }
```
But if both queries apply (height 320), the later max-height 380 overrides pause/end. Good.

Let's implement. Also need ensure `.screen` transform doesn't affect centering? The screen is flex with align-items center/justify-content center. The card's layout center is at viewport center. Transform origin default center. Good.

Could alternatively use `scale` property but transform is fine. Add `transform-origin: center center;` optional.

Let's patch.

